1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,040
Listen to a talk in an anthropology class.

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:09,200
The potlatch is a ceremonial event among Indigenous peoples of the Pacific Northwest coast of North America.

3
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:12,895
A potlatch involves the host giving away their possessions,

4
00:00:12,895 --> 00:00:16,180
including food, blankets, and even money, to guests.

5
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:19,708
This practice serves multiple social functions,

6
00:00:19,708 --> 00:00:25,000
like redistributing wealth, reinforcing social bonds, and asserting status.

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,453
Potlatches are often held to mark significant occasions,

8
00:00:28,453 --> 00:00:33,200
like weddings and such, and they involve feasting, dancing, and storytelling.

9
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:40,030
The lavishness of a potlatch can elevate the host's status by demonstrating wealth and generosity.

10
00:00:40,030 --> 00:00:44,087
One fascinating aspect of the potlatch is its role in the social

11
00:00:44,087 --> 00:00:47,130
and economic structures of the communities that practice it.

12
00:00:47,130 --> 00:00:51,913
By redistributing wealth, potlatches ensure that resources circulate within the community,

13
00:00:51,913 --> 00:00:57,130
preventing the concentration of wealth in the hands of a few individuals.

14
00:00:57,130 --> 00:01:02,290
This practice strengthens community ties and fosters mutual support.

15
00:01:02,290 --> 00:01:06,040
For people who don't understand the function of potlatches

16
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:09,000
they may appear to be a waste of resources.

17
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:14,019
Potlatches were even prohibited by the Canadian government in the late-nineteenth century

18
00:01:14,019 --> 00:01:16,110
because of this perceived wastefulness.

19
00:01:16,110 --> 00:01:19,221
The ban was lifted in the mid-twentieth century,

20
00:01:19,221 --> 00:01:23,110
and today, potlatches continue to be an important cultural practice.

21
00:01:23,110 --> 00:01:27,690
Let's now examine how Indigenous communities have revived this practice.
